          id: 41,
          extra: `<p><b>Part 2. Read the passage and choose the correct answer (A, B, C or D) to each of the questions</b></p><p>The teenage years, often referred to as adolescence, are a pivotal period in a person's life. This stage marks the transition from childhood to adulthood, and it's characterized by physical, emotional, and social changes. Let's explore the unique challenges and experiences that teenagers face during this transformative phase.<br>
One of the most noticeable changes during adolescence is the rapid physical development. Teenagers undergo significant growth spurts, which may result in awkwardness as they adjust to their changing bodies. This period also brings about the onset of puberty, a process marked by the development of secondary sexual characteristics. These physical changes can be both exciting and confusing for teenagers, as they come to terms with their emerging identities. <br>
Emotionally, teenagers often <b><u>grapple with</u></b> a rollercoaster of feelings. Hormonal fluctuations can lead to mood swings, making it challenging for them to regulate their emotions. They may also experience a heightened sense of self-awareness and self-consciousness, which can sometimes result in self-esteem issues. Peer pressure becomes more influential, and teenagers may feel the need to fit in, even if it means making choices they might later regret. <br>
Socially, adolescents begin to explore their independence and identity. They seek greater autonomy from their parents and strive to establish their own identities. Friendships become increasingly important, and teenagers may start forming closer bonds with peers who share <b><u>their</u></b> interests and values. However, these newfound social connections can also lead to conflicts and peer pressure, making it crucial for teenagers to develop strong decision-making skills. <br><div style="text-align: right;"><i>(Adapted from Verywell Mind)</i></div></p>`,
